    <string name="help_title">Set up Gmail</string>
    <string name="help_intro">Photo to Mail sends your photos through a Gmail account. You set this up once; allow about 5 minutes. Use a computer or this phone.</string>
    <string name="step1_t">Gmail account</string>
    <string name="step1_b">You need a Gmail address to send from. If you don\'t have one, create a free account at gmail.com.</string>
    <string name="step2_t">Turn on 2-Step Verification</string>
    <string name="step2_b">Go to myaccount.google.com → Security → 2-Step Verification and turn it on. Without this step you cannot create an app password.</string>
    <string name="open_security">Open Google security</string>
    <string name="step3_t">Create an app password</string>
    <string name="step3_b">Go to myaccount.google.com/apppasswords. Enter \"Photo to Mail\" as the name and tap Create. You get a 16-letter code (e.g. abcd efgh ijkl mnop). Copy it right away: Google shows it only once.</string>
    <string name="open_apppw">Open app passwords</string>
    <string name="step4_t">Enter the details in the app</string>
    <string name="step4_b">Open Settings and fill in:\n•  Your Gmail address (sender)\n•  The 16-letter app password (spaces are fine)\n•  Send to: the address the photos should go to, e.g. your work email\nThen tap Save.</string>
    <string name="step5_t">Test</string>
    <string name="step5_b">In Settings, tap Send test email. Check that the email arrives; also check the Spam or Junk folder.</string>
    <string name="good_to_know">Good to know</string>
    <string name="tips">•  Never use your normal Gmail password; it does not work here.\n•  The app password stays on this phone only.\n•  Lost your phone? Remove the app password at myaccount.google.com/apppasswords.\n•  Message \"Sign-in failed\"? Check the Gmail address and create a new app password if needed.\n•  Photos are reduced by default; emails are at most 15 MB.</string>
